# Clear typology error

Harbor State Bank is filing this SAR on customer Anthony Rivera, age 36, of Newark, New Jersey, regarding suspected structuring and possible money laundering activity involving personal checking account 30988421. Rivera opened the account on March 8, 2025. At account opening, Rivera identified his occupation as an operations manager for a regional logistics company with annual income of approximately $88,000. He stated that the account would be used primarily for payroll deposits, household expenses, and routine personal purchases, with expected monthly activity generally below $12,000.

From May 4, 2026, through July 22, 2026, Rivera received 19 incoming electronic transfers totaling $171,650 from 13 individuals located in New Jersey, New York, Pennsylvania, and Connecticut. The transfers were received through ACH and person-to-person payment services and ranged from $6,900 to $9,850 each. No individual transfer exceeded $10,000.

The senders included Melissa Grant, Patrick Lee, Angela Morrison, David Shah, Carlos Vega, Lauren Kim, and seven additional individuals. Review of available customer and transaction records identified no apparent family, employment, or business relationship between Rivera and the majority of the senders. The payment descriptions included generic terms such as “invoice,” “services,” “payment,” and “consulting,” despite Rivera having no known business activity reflected in his customer profile.

The electronic transfers were received into Rivera’s personal checking account through online banking and electronic payment channels. The activity was associated primarily with IP addresses resolving to northern New Jersey. Rivera did not make corresponding cash deposits during the review period.

Between May 6 and July 25, 2026, Rivera initiated eight outgoing international wire transfers totaling $158,900 to Everbright Trading Co. Ltd., an electronics wholesaler with an account at Eastern Pacific Commercial Bank in Hong Kong. Individual outgoing wires ranged from $14,500 to $27,800. Six of the wires occurred within three business days of Rivera receiving multiple incoming electronic payments.

For example, on June 11, 2026, Rivera received an $8,750 ACH credit from Melissa Grant and a $9,200 person-to-person transfer from Patrick Lee. On June 13, 2026, Rivera initiated a $17,600 international wire to Everbright Trading Co. Ltd. On June 27, 2026, Rivera received electronic payments of $7,800 from Angela Morrison, $9,450 from David Shah, and $8,900 from Carlos Vega. On June 29, 2026, Rivera sent a $24,300 international wire to Everbright Trading Co. Ltd.

Historical account review from March 2025 through April 2026 showed Rivera primarily receiving biweekly payroll deposits ranging from $2,900 to $3,200 from his stated employer. His prior outgoing activity consisted primarily of mortgage payments, utilities, credit card payments, retail purchases, and domestic electronic transfers. The account had no prior history of international wires, commercial payments, or receipt of multiple payments from unrelated individuals.

On July 10, 2026, Harbor State Bank contacted Rivera regarding the increased electronic activity and international wires. Rivera stated that he had recently begun helping friends and acquaintances purchase consumer electronics from an overseas supplier and that the incoming payments represented reimbursement for merchandise. Rivera stated that he did not consider the activity a business and had not established a separate business entity or business bank account.

Rivera provided two invoices from Everbright Trading Co. Ltd. showing bulk purchases of mobile phones and laptop computers. The invoices corresponded generally to two of the international wires but did not identify the individuals who had sent funds to Rivera or explain how the amounts received from individual senders related to specific merchandise purchases. Rivera did not provide purchase orders, shipping records, resale records, or documentation showing delivery of merchandise to the individuals who funded the account.

Open-source review identified Everbright Trading Co. Ltd. as an active Hong Kong company associated with wholesale electronics. Harbor State Bank identified no adverse media or sanctions-related information concerning the company during its review. Open-source review of Rivera did not identify a registered business, commercial website, professional profile, or other public indication that he operated an electronics purchasing or resale business.

Harbor State Bank considers the activity suspicious because Rivera received repeated electronic payments below $10,000 from multiple apparently unrelated individuals and subsequently transferred most of the funds to an overseas commercial beneficiary. The activity represents a significant change from Rivera’s historical account activity and is inconsistent with the personal use and employment information provided at account opening.

Harbor State Bank believes the repeated incoming electronic transfers below $10,000 may represent structuring designed to avoid Currency Transaction Report filing requirements. The subsequent international wires may also indicate movement or layering of funds through Rivera’s personal account.

The bank placed Rivera’s account under enhanced monitoring on July 26, 2026. The account remains open. Harbor State Bank has not identified cash deposits or cash withdrawals associated with the activity described in this filing. Account statements, electronic transfer records, wire records, customer communications, invoices provided by Rivera, customer due diligence records, and IP information are available upon request.
